Consider the following code:

int pid = fork();
if (pid > 0) {
    printf("Parent: child PID is %d\n", pid);
} else if (pid == 0) {
    printf("Child: my PID is %d\n", getpid());
}

How many printf statements will execute?

Consider this code:

int x = 5;
fork();
x = x + 1;
printf("%d\n", x);

Assuming fork() succeeds, how many times will the printf statement execute and what values will be printed?

Consider:

int main() {
    fork();
    fork();
    printf("Process\n");
    return 0;
}

How many times will "Process" be printed?

Consider the following code:

char *argv[] = {"ls", "-l", NULL};
execvp(argv[0], argv);
printf("After exec\n");

What will happen?

Consider this code:
```c
int status;
pid_t child_pid = fork();
if (child_pid == 0) {
    exit(5);
} else {
    wait(&status);
    int exit_code = WEXITSTATUS(status);
}
```
What is the value of exit_code in the parent process?

Consider this code snippet:
```c
int x = 5;
void* thread_func(void* arg) {
    x = x + 10;
    return NULL;
}
int main() {
    pthread_t tid;
    pthread_create(&tid, NULL, thread_func, NULL);
    pthread_join(tid, NULL);
    printf("%d\n", x);
}
```
What will be printed?

What is the expected output of this program?
```c
int counter = 0;
void* increment(void* arg) {
    counter++;
    return NULL;
}
int main() {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, increment, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, increment, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);
    printf("%d\n", counter);
}
```

Consider this code with multiple threads modifying a shared array:
```c
int arr[100] = {0};
void* fill_array(void* arg) {
    int idx = (int)(intptr_t)arg;
    for(int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        arr[idx]++;
    }
    return NULL;
}
```
If we create 10 threads (each with different idx from 0-9) and join all of them, what can we say with certainty about arr[0] through arr[9]?

What is the critical issue with this code?
```c
void* thread_func(void* arg) {
    int* ptr = (int*)arg;
    printf("%d\n", *ptr);
    return NULL;
}
int main() {
    pthread_t tid;
    int x = 42;
    pthread_create(&tid, NULL, thread_func, &x);
    return 0;  // main exits without joining
}
```

Analyze this scenario: Two threads concurrently execute the following without synchronization:
```c
int shared = 0;
// Thread A: shared = shared + 1; shared = shared + 1;
// Thread B: shared = shared + 2; shared = shared + 2;
```
Assuming each statement executes atomically within the thread but threads can interleave, what value(s) are possible for shared after both threads complete?

Consider this code snippet:
```c
int counter = 0;
void *increment(void *arg) {
  for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    counter++;
  }
  return NULL;
}
```
If two threads execute this function concurrently without synchronisation, what is the most likely final value of counter?

Consider two threads executing the following code with a shared variable `balance = 100` and an unprotected mutex pattern:
```c
pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
int temp = balance;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
temp -= 50;
pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
balance = temp;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
```
If Thread A reads balance=100 but before it updates, Thread B also reads balance=100, modifies it to 150, and writes back, what will be the final balance?
